And second, it is
not in general the case that every part of an object’s causal history inheres in that
object. A novelist having written a book while listening to classical music would not
make that book a piece of music, let alone a piece of classical music; and this
is the case whether or not the piece of music caused the novelist to write the
book. Objects are forgetful in this way, and perhaps this justifies a certain amount
of envy toward them.
—Oliver Traldi

